<h2>The corporate insolvency backdrop</h2>
<p>Manjeera Constructions Ltd was undergoing corporate insolvency resolution as corporate debtor and guarantor. The Committee of Creditors approved the resolution plan submitted by Valentis Laboratories (in consortium with Surya Treasure Island) with a commanding 91.64% voting share. S2 Tech, a financial creditor with 3.3%, objected; the NCLT rejected its application on 30 July 2024 and went on to approve the plan on 26 March 2025.</p>
<p>S2 Tech appealed to the NCLAT under Section 61 of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code — but did not implead Valentis, the successful resolution applicant. On 19 March 2026, the NCLAT nonetheless delivered a judgment invalidating the plan, apparently in ignorance of the fact that the plan had meanwhile been approved.</p>

<h2>The Supreme Court&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Direct impact, absent party.</strong> The plan&rsquo;s approval had a direct bearing on the appeal, and its author — the successful resolution applicant — was not before the tribunal.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Flagrant breach.</strong> Deciding against that absent party&rsquo;s core commercial interest was, in the Court&rsquo;s words, a flagrant violation of audi alteram partem — enough by itself to make the judgment unsustainable.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Set aside, no remand.</strong> The NCLAT judgment of 19 March 2026 was set aside. Rather than remand, the Court channelled every live issue into the pending appeals against the NCLT&rsquo;s approval order of 26 March 2025, where all parties are arrayed.</div>
</div>
<p>The judgment, authored by Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Sanjay Kumar, is deliberately narrow: no view on the plan&rsquo;s validity, the dissenting creditor&rsquo;s objections or the earnest-money question. Everything substantive remains open — before the right forum, with the right parties.</p>

<h2>Why the discipline matters in insolvency</h2>
<p>Resolution plans are not ordinary litigation outcomes; they are implemented commercial transactions. By the time an appeal matures, the successful applicant may have infused funds, taken over management and begun performing the plan. A judgment unwinding all of that, rendered without the applicant in the room, maximises precisely the uncertainty the Code was designed to eliminate. The Supreme Court&rsquo;s refusal to remand is equally pragmatic: rather than reopen a defective appeal, it consolidated the controversy in the pending appeals where the record and the parties are complete — the fastest lawful route to finality.</p>

<h2>Checklist for IBC appellate practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Array of parties first: the resolution professional, the CoC where necessary, and always the successful resolution applicant once a plan exists. Objectors who omit the winner build appeals on sand.</p>
  <p>Keep the appellate tribunal informed of developments below — here, the NCLAT acted in ignorance of the plan&rsquo;s approval, a failure of the record as much as of procedure.</p>
  <p>Minority dissent has remedies, but they run through the Code&rsquo;s own channels: objections under Section 30(6) scrutiny, and appeals against the approval order itself.</p>
  <p>Where a judgment in your favour was obtained without a necessary party, treat it as fragile: the defect is jurisdictional in character and travels with the order.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment for general information. The underlying appeals against the plan-approval order remain pending, and nothing here comments on their merits.</div>
